# Shortcodes

@lede
The single extension point for the language. They replace inline HTML.
Two forms, registered names, validated arguments.
@end

## The two forms

@dl
Inline
: `@name(args)[content]` — appears inside a paragraph, heading, list item,
  table cell, or other inline context.
Block
: `@name(args)` ... `@end` — opens on its own line, has a body parsed as
  full blocks (recursively), closes with `@end` on its own line at the
  same indentation.
@end

## Inline form

```brief
See @link[the spec](https://example.com/brief) for details.
Press @kbd[Ctrl+C] to abort.
The energy is @math[E = m c^2].
```

The `[...]` body is parsed as inline content. Argument syntax is in §3
below.

## Block form

```brief
@callout(kind: warning)
Watch out for @kbd[Shift+Delete] on Linux. It can wipe data without going
through the trash.
@end
```

Block bodies parse recursively — a callout can contain paragraphs, lists,
nested blocks, and other shortcodes.

## Argument syntax

Arguments inside `(...)` are positional or keyword. Mixed usage is
allowed, with positionals first:

```brief
@callout(kind: warning)
@image(src: "diagram.png", alt: "architecture diagram")[]
@link[GitHub](https://github.com/kirillleventcov/brief)
```

Argument values are one of:

- Bare identifiers: `kind: warning`
- Integers: `width: 800`
- Quoted strings: `src: "diagram.png"`
- Arrays: `align: [left, right, center]`

The compiler validates each invocation against the registered shortcode:
required arguments, types, allowed values (the `oneof` constraint). A
type mismatch is @ec[B0402]; a required-argument omission is @ec[B0403];
a `oneof` violation is @ec[B0404].

## Registration

All shortcodes must be registered. Using an unregistered name is @ec[B0401].

The reference compiler ships @ref[reference/built-in-shortcodes.brf](built-ins)
covering links, images, keyboard input, sub/superscript, collapsible
details, callouts, math, footnotes, definition lists, tables, code, and
cross-document references.

User-defined shortcodes live in `brief.toml`:

```toml
[shortcodes.note]
kind = "block"
template_html = "<aside class=\"note\">{{content}}</aside>"
template_llm  = "[note] {{content}} [/note]"
```

See @ref[reference/brief-toml.brf](brief.toml) for the full
configuration grammar.

## Self-terminating shortcodes

Two shortcodes do not require `@end`:

- `@t` (tables) — terminates at the first non-`|` line.
- The triple-backtick code fence — terminates at its closing fence.

User-defined shortcodes can *never* be self-terminating. The restriction
is permanent.

## Why no inline HTML

A markup language whose extension story is "drop in arbitrary HTML"
trades determinism for flexibility, and Brief is an explicit refusal of
that trade. Shortcodes give the same flexibility under structural
constraints: the parser stays context-free, the AST stays clean, and
non-HTML targets (LLM, JSON-AST) get a real translation, not a
passthrough.

## A note on the `[content]` body

Inline shortcodes have a *single-line* `[content]` body. A line break
inside `[...]` is invalid. If you need block content, use the block form.

## In LLM mode

Shortcodes render via their `template_llm` if registered, or to a compact
default if not. Some examples:

@t
| Source | LLM-mode output
| `@link[text](url)` | `[text](url)`
| `@image(src, alt)` | `[image: alt]`
| `@callout(kind: warning) ... @end` | `[!warning] ... [/!]`
| `@math[x^2]` | `$x^2$`
| `@footnote[body]` | `[^1]` inline; definition appended at end
| `@ref[path#anchor](text)` | `text` — the link target is dropped
